{"id":993,"date":"2020-11-17T11:19:19","date_gmt":"2020-11-17T19:19:19","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=993"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:17:11","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:17:11","slug":"codrone-arduino-flight-movements","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-flight-movements\/","title":{"rendered":"Flight Movements"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c134391\" name=\"6a2215c134391\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Air vehicles move a little differently than land vehicles. While land vehicles can move forward, backward, and sometimes side-to-side, air vehicles can do all of that and move up and down. To keep track of how a CoDrone moves in a three-dimensional space, you can use the terms below:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Roll<\/strong>\u00a0controls the CoDrone&#8217;s horizontal, or side to side, movement. Positive roll will make the CoDrone move to the right, and negative roll will make the drone move to the left.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;955&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Pitch<\/strong>\u00a0is the CoDrone&#8217;s forward and backward tilt. Positive pitch will make the CoDrone tilt and move forward, and negative pitch will make the CoDrone tilt and move backwards.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;958&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Yaw<\/strong>\u00a0is the CoDrone&#8217;s left and right rotation. Positive yaw will make the CoDrone turn to the right, and negative yaw will make the CoDrone turn to the left.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;959&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Throttle<\/strong>\u00a0controls the CoDrone&#8217;s vertical, or up and down, movement. Positive throttle will make the CoDrone fly higher, and negative throttle will make the CoDrone fly lower.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;960&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Be careful with yaw and roll! Yaw will make your CoDrone turn left or right, while roll will move your CoDrone left or right.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c134507\" name=\"6a2215c134507\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Activity: Blind drone<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]One of the best ways to learn new words is to see them in action, so instead of you just memorizing all of the flight movements, you&#8217;re going to do an obstacle course!<\/p>\n<p>Have a friend sit down on a chair and then blindfold them (or just have them keep their eyes closed). Next, guide them through an obstacle course to have them sit down on another chair at the end using only flight movement words. Make sure that anything dangerous is out of their way first!<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive throttle: stand up<\/li>\n<li>Negative throttle: sit down<\/li>\n<li>Positive pitch: move forward<\/li>\n<li>Negative pitch: move backward<\/li>\n<li>Positive yaw: turn right<\/li>\n<li>Negative yaw: turn left<\/li>\n<li>Positive roll: move right<\/li>\n<li>Negative roll: turn left<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For example, if you wanted to tell your friend to move forward, you could say, &#8220;Positive pitch 2 steps.&#8221; That would let your friend know that you want them to move forward by 2 steps. Once your friend successfully completes the obstacle course, switch roles so they can give you directions![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c134686\" name=\"6a2215c134686\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Movement commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There are three steps to make your CoDrone fly autonomously:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Set movement commands<\/li>\n<li>Send commands<\/li>\n<li>Set movement duration<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Movement commands are what you just learned about: yaw, pitch, roll, and throttle. To set their speed values in Arduino, use the following format:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\nCoDrone.setPitch(0);\r\nCoDrone.setRoll(0);\r\nCoDrone.setYaw(0);<\/pre>\n<p>The values for these numbers can range from -100 to 100. For example, if you wanted to set throttle to 70, you would write <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.setThrottle(70)<\/code>. This saves the throttle&#8217;s speed value as 70, but it doesn&#8217;t send the command to your CoDrone&#8230;yet.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c13471e\" name=\"6a2215c13471e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Movement duration<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The last thing you need to do is give your flight movements time to play out. This is how you would use it with multiple flight commands:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setRoll(60);\r\nCoDrone.setPitch(-60);\r\nCoDrone.move(1.5);<\/pre>\n<p>This <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.move(1.5)<\/code>will pause your code for 1.5 seconds after you send your flight commands. This will give your CoDrone 1.5 seconds to execute the commands before it moves on to the next line of code.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;994&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c1347a8\" name=\"6a2215c1347a8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now you get to put it all together! Have your CoDrone take off, throttle at a speed of 50 for 2 seconds, fly forward and to the left at a speed of 30 for 2 seconds, and then land. Don&#8217;t forget to include a kill switch in void loop!<\/p>\n<p>Your code should look something like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n    CoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();          \/\/Take off for 3 seconds\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(50);            \/\/Throttle for 2 seconds\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(0);              \/\/ Set throttle back to 0\r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(30);\r\n    CoDrone.setRoll(-30);                \/\/Pitch and roll for 2 seconds\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.land();          \/\/Land\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop () {\r\n    byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n    byte bt4 = digitalRead(14);\r\n    byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n\r\n    if(bt1 &amp;&amp; bt4 &amp;&amp; !bt8){\r\n        CoDrone.emergencyStop();\r\n    }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Set baud rate to 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a nearby drone.<\/li>\n<li>Take off for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>Throttle (fly up) at a speed of 50 for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>Positive pitch (fly forward) and negative roll (fly to the left) at a speed of 30 for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>Land.<\/li>\n<li>If sensors 11 and 14 are covered, stop the motors.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215c134858\" name=\"6a2215c134858\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p><strong>Challenge: Pin Point<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Set up a starting pad and a landing pad at least a few feet apart. Try to get the CoDrone to fly from one pad to the next using your own autonomous program.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The CoDrone must start within the starting pad and end within the landing pad.<\/li>\n<li>The CoDrone has to move on its own &#8212; no joysticks on the remote control!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>If you want to make the challenge more difficult, you can:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Have the CoDrone face away from the landing pad.<\/li>\n<li>Add a hoop or obstacle that the CoDrone has to fly through or around.<\/li>\n<li>Turn the target into a bullseye and give each section a different point value. The middle of the board should have the highest number of points, and the edges of the board should have the least amount of points.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle [vc_column_text]Air vehicles move a little differently than land vehicles. While land vehicles can move forward, backward, and sometimes side-to-side, air vehicles can&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-flight-movements\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1751,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1438,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2163,2135,2164,2165,2115,2123,2122],"class_list":["post-993","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-flight-directions","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/993","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/993\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":995,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/993\/revisions\/995"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1751"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=993"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=993"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=993"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}